WEBVTT

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:05.547
Tokens.

00:00:05.867 --> 00:00:07.488
Cutting text into crates.

00:00:08.088 --> 00:00:12.215
Everything that comes through my customs shed goes into a standard crate.

00:00:12.535 --> 00:00:15.629
Text too, before any language model can touch it.

00:00:16.829 --> 00:00:18.808
A language model doesn't read letters.

00:00:19.128 --> 00:00:23.318
It reads a list of pieces, each one a number from its vocabulary.

00:00:23.638 --> 00:00:26.164
The question is how big to cut the pieces.

00:00:26.764 --> 00:00:40.153
Cut by letter, with one extra mark for the end of each word, and the guides' sentences take about 4.39 pieces per word, counting each full stop and question mark as a word: tiny crates, a great many trips.

00:00:40.753 --> 00:00:43.343
Cut by whole word, and every word is one piece.

00:00:43.663 --> 00:00:47.347
But a word the model has never seen has no crate at all.

00:00:48.547 --> 00:00:50.800
Byte-pair encoding meets in the middle.

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:53.436
Start from single letters.

00:00:53.756 --> 00:00:56.913
Count every pair of neighbouring pieces, inside words.

00:00:57.233 --> 00:00:59.718
The most common pair gets a crate of its own.

00:01:00.038 --> 00:01:01.175
Then count again.

00:01:01.775 --> 00:01:09.122
On the guides' sentences, the first merge joins an e to the end-of-word mark, so an e that ends a word gets one crate.

00:01:09.442 --> 00:01:13.589
By the fifth merge, the whole word 'the' has its own crate.

00:01:14.189 --> 00:01:16.947
After fifty merges, how many pieces does a word take?

00:01:19.447 --> 00:01:23.679
After 50 merges, a word takes 1.80 pieces on average.

00:01:23.999 --> 00:01:28.104
After 136, every one of the guides' words is a single piece.

00:01:29.304 --> 00:01:32.146
Now a word that never came through the shed: lamplit.

00:01:32.466 --> 00:01:33.897
There's no crate for it.

00:01:34.497 --> 00:01:40.140
So it's cut into pieces that do have crates: l, am, p, and lit at the end of a word.

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:41.786
Nothing is thrown away.

00:01:42.106 --> 00:01:50.106
Rico Sennrich and his colleagues used this method, in a 2016 paper, to translate rare words.

00:01:51.306 --> 00:01:54.569
The guides only know 44 words and 2 marks.

00:01:54.889 --> 00:02:01.731
So here's real English: the opening of Moby-Dick, about 599 words of it, to learn the merges from.

00:02:02.331 --> 00:02:05.868
Then a page it never saw, the opening of Pride and Prejudice.

00:02:06.188 --> 00:02:10.883
With 400 merges, the new page takes 2.14 pieces per word.

00:02:11.203 --> 00:02:15.876
The word lighthouse, never seen, comes out as li, gh, thou and se.

00:02:17.076 --> 00:02:18.992
Does it matter which pairs we merge?

00:02:19.312 --> 00:02:29.396
Merge random pairs instead, the same number of times, and over ten tries the guides' words still take 1.32 to 1.67 pieces each, instead of one.

00:02:30.596 --> 00:02:33.775
There's one more choice: what the very first pieces are.

00:02:34.095 --> 00:02:35.590
Characters run out fast.

00:02:35.910 --> 00:02:45.741
The page from Pride and Prejudice has curly quotation marks, and a capital D and E, that Moby-Dick never used, so with characters as the base, they have no crate.

00:02:46.341 --> 00:02:48.762
GPT-2 starts from bytes instead.

00:02:49.082 --> 00:02:53.840
There are only 256 of them, and any text at all is some list of bytes.

00:02:54.160 --> 00:03:02.055
An a is one byte, an e with its accent built in is two, a curly apostrophe three, and a seal emoji four.

00:03:02.655 --> 00:03:08.444
Start from bytes, and that new page has 0 pieces without a crate, instead of 16.

00:03:09.644 --> 00:03:13.750
The little language model from the attention lesson skips all of this.

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:21.164
It has one crate per word, 46 of them counting the full stop and question mark, because its whole world is those 46.

00:03:21.764 --> 00:03:32.543
Byte-pair encoding needs 136 merges, and a vocabulary of 160, to cut the same sentences into whole words, because every piece it made on the way stays in the vocabulary.

00:03:33.743 --> 00:03:41.112
Common pairs get their own crates, rare words come apart into pieces, and bytes mean nothing is ever left on the dock.

00:03:41.712 --> 00:03:44.596
Next: giving every crate a place on the chart.

00:03:44.916 --> 00:03:45.653
Goodnight.

